    "title": {
     "en": "A short story"
    },
    "text": {
     "en": "A poster claimed 'nine out of ten doctors recommend this toothpaste'.\n\"Who did they ask?\" wondered Ravi."
    }
   },
   {
    "id": "explain",
    "type": "explanation",
    "provenance": "original",
    "keepEnglishTerms": [
     "data",
     "representative value",
     "misleading"
    ],
    "text": {
     "en": "Data begins with a question. We choose whom to ask and how, collect answers and make statements. Good questions are clear and fair.\nRepresentative values such as the mean, median and mode summarise data. Graphs help us see it, but graphs can mislead if scales or ends are changed.\nAlways ask: Who collected this? How many were asked? Is the scale fair? This is how we spot tall tales."
    }
   },
